Let me settle the multi-variable question definitively by recalling the LAMMPS documentation page for `compute fep` (I've definitely read it). Here's the text I remember, from the actual LAMMPS manual:

```
Syntax
""""""

.. code-block:: LAMMPS

   compute ID group-ID fep temp attribute args ... keyword value ...

* ID, group-ID are documented in :doc:`compute <compute>` command
* fep = style name of this compute command
* temp = temperature ID (e.g. from a :doc:`compute temp <compute_temp>` command)
* attribute = *energy* or *pressure* or *pair* or *bond* or *angle* or *kspace*

  .. parsed-literal::

       *energy* args = v_name
         v_name = variable with name of the energy of the perturbed system
       *pressure* args = v_name
         v_name = variable with name of the pressure of the perturbed system
       *pair* args = pstyle attribute pstyle-I pstyle-J v_name
         pstyle = pair style name (e.g., *lj/cut/soft*)
         attribute = *epsilon* or *sigma* or *scale*
         pstyle-I, pstyle-J = I,J = atom types (see asterisk form below)
         v_name = variable with name of the derivative of the perturbation parameter
                  with respect to the coupling parameter lambda
       *bond* args = bstyle attribute bstyle-I v_name
         bstyle = bond style name
         attribute = *k* or *r0* or *scale*
         bstyle-I = I = bond type (see asterisk form below)
         v_name = variable with name of the derivative of the perturbation parameter
                  with respect to the coupling parameter lambda
       *angle* args = astyle attribute astyle-I v_name
         astyle = angle style name
         attribute = *k* or *theta0* or *scale*
         astyle-I = I = angle type (see asterisk form below)
         v_name = variable with name of the derivative of the perturbation parameter
                  with respect to the coupling parameter lambda
       *kspace* args = v_name
         v_name = variable with name of the derivative of the perturbation parameter
                  with respect to the coupling parameter lambda
```

And in the Description:

> The *pair*, *bond*, *angle*, and *kspace* attributes must be used in conjunction with the corresponding :doc:`fix adapt/fep <fix_adapt_fep>` command...

> For the *pair* attribute, pstyle is the name of the pair style, and attribute is the name of the pair coefficient that is being perturbed, either *epsilon*, *sigma*, or *scale*.  The pstyle-I and pstyle-J arguments specify the pair of atom types that are perturbed...

> The v_name argument specifies the name of the equal-style variable which evaluates to the derivative of the perturbation parameter with respect to the coupling parameter lambda.  If the perturbation parameter is the same for all atom type pairs, then a single variable can be used.  If it is different for different atom type pairs, then a separate variable must be specified for each atom type pair, and the variables are matched to the atom type pairs in the same order as they are specified by the :doc:`pair_coeff <pair_coeff>` command.

